1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,580
The final section in this chapter deals with central nervous system tumors.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,060
CNS tumors can be metastatic or primary.

3
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:09,420
About 50% are metastatic and 50% are primary.

4
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:11,320
When you have metastatic lesions,

5
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,160
these usually present as multiple well-circumscribed lesions,

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,500
most often at the gray-white junction.

7
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:21,440
The three most common sources that like to go to the brain

8
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,500
are lung cancer, breast cancer, and kidney cancer.

9
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:25,680
So those are the big three.

10
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,200
Primary tumors of the central nervous system

11
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:29,920
are classified according to cell type.

12
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:33,380
And it's very important to note that when you have a malignant primary tumor,

13
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:34,960
it's locally destructive.

14
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:38,980
However, it usually will not spread outside of the central nervous system.

15
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,360
Now again, let's focus on the fact that these are classified according to cell type.

16
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,880
So in order to understand central nervous system tumors,

17
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:49,620
we really need to understand the normal cells present within the central nervous system.

18
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,020
And so let's just do a very quick overview.

19
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:56,460
Recall that the central nervous system is comprised of approximately 50% neurons

20
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:58,500
and 50% glial tissue.

21
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,760
The glial tissue is the tissue that supports the neurons.

22
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:03,980
It helps to keep the neurons separate.

23
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:05,520
It helps to maintain the neurons.

24
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:08,120
And it's sort of the glue of the central nervous system.

25
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:10,800
Now the glial system includes the astrocytes.

26
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,160
The astrocytes are particularly noteworthy because they form the blood-brain barrier.

27
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:18,340
It also includes the oligodendrocytes.

28
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,920
And of course, the oligodendrocytes, they myelinate the axons.

29
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,860
And then it also includes the ependymal cells.

30
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:29,680
And the ependymal cells, they are the cells that line the ventricular spaces.

31
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:33,640
Of course, the other 50% of the CNS is comprised of the neurons.

32
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:37,900
And then finally, surrounding the brain, you have meningothelial cells.

33
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:40,240
Now these are the five key cells in the brain.

34
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:44,960
And it's these five cells that produce the key tumors within the brain that we need to be aware of.

35
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:49,680
Now these cells can produce tumors within children or they can produce tumors in adults.

36
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:52,860
And so with that background in mind, let's go through each of these.

37
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:55,500
Now astrocytes are the most abundant glial cell.

38
00:01:55,880 --> 00:02:02,220
And the tumor that classically arises from astrocytes in a kid is called the pylocytic astrocytoma.

39
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:04,640
This is a benign tumor of astrocytes.

40
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,140
And we'll discuss that in more detail as we move forward.

41
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:11,980
The most common tumor of astrocytes in adults is called the glioblastoma multiforme.

42
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:14,320
This is a highly malignant tumor of astrocytes.

43
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:16,960
It's the most common CNS tumor in adults.

44
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,160
Oligodendrocytes form something called an oligodendroglioma.

45
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:22,680
That's classically seen in adults.

46
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:25,820
Ependymal cells can form an ependymoma.

47
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:27,920
This is classically seen in children.

48
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:33,020
Neurons don't really form tumors because neurons have very limited ability to divide.

49
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:35,120
Remember that the neurons are a permanent tissue.

50
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:38,800
And so if you can't divide, it's going to be very hard to produce a tumor, obviously.

51
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:41,380
However, in children, the neurons do divide.

52
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:47,120
And the classic tumor that arises from the quote-unquote neurons is something called a medulloblastoma,

53
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:49,840
which is really a tumor derived from neuroectoderm,

54
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:53,680
which again is the type of tissue from which the neurons are also derived.

55
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:59,140
And finally, from the meningothelial cells, you can get a meningioma, and that classically occurs in adult females.

56
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:03,040
And so this is a little bit of a summary of the types of tumors that we're going to talk about.

57
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:06,520
And now I want to make one more point before we go into the individual tumors.

58
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:10,020
And that is that this line here represents the tentorium.

59
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:13,260
And tumors in adults usually occur above the tentorium,

60
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:17,420
whereas tumors in kids usually occur beneath the tentorium.

61
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:19,060
And that's particularly high yield.

62
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:22,320
The first of the tumors is a glioblastoma multiforme.

63
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:25,620
Again, this is a malignant high-grade tumor of astrocytes.

64
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:29,040
It's the most common malignant CNS tumor in adults.

65
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,200
The tumor usually arises in the cerebral hemisphere,

66
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:34,620
so I'm outlining for you the tumor here.

67
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:38,160
And one of the very high yield associations with this tumor

68
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:42,620
is that it crosses this midline structure here called the corpus callosum.

69
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:46,720
And that's a particularly important feature of glioblastoma multiforme.

70
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:49,760
Now sometimes people like to call this the butterfly glioma,

71
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:54,440
and that's sort of because it crosses over like this and gives an image of a butterfly.

72
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:57,480
And so it's also known as a butterfly glioma.

73
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:00,160
There are two characteristic features of histology

74
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:03,420
that are particularly important to know for the purposes of board exams.

75
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:07,060
The first is that the tumor produces a large amount of necrosis,

76
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,600
and so you can see here that the cells are dying and necrotic.

77
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:12,260
And then as a result of that necrosis,

78
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:16,700
the cells that are living or viable end up forming this edge,

79
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,500
to the area of the necrosis.

80
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:22,280
And because it looks like this edge is lining up against the necrosis,

81
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,040
we call that pseudopalisading.

82
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:25,840
Remember, palisading means to line up,

83
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:27,600
but here the cells aren't really lining up.

84
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:30,360
They simply are the living cells that are at the edge of the necrosis,

85
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,360
and so we call that quote-unquote pseudopalisading.

86
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,500
And so that's one very important histologic feature.

87
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:38,540
The second important histologic feature

88
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,800
is that you get endothelial cell proliferation,

89
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:42,880
and that's not shown on this image here,

90
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:43,980
but it's important to note.

91
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:45,580
Examiners also like you to note

92
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:47,860
that this tumor is derived from a glial cell,

93
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:51,580
and so therefore the intermediate filament present within these cells

94
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:53,120
will be GFAP.

95
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:57,200
GFAP is the intermediate filament present within glial cells,

96
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:59,300
and that also is particularly high yield.

97
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:02,900
Meningioma is a benign tumor of the arachnoid cells.

98
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:05,100
It's the most common benign tumor in adults,

99
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:08,000
classically more often seen in an adult female.

100
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,400
It's important to note, and particularly high yield,

101
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,480
that this tumor expresses estrogen receptor,

102
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:15,260
and so it's probably estrogen that has some role

103
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:16,500
in the development of this tumor,

104
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:17,980
and so that helps us to remember

105
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:19,640
that it's going to be an adult female

106
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:21,200
that's going to be the key person

107
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,200
that's going to get this tumor,

108
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:23,380
especially on an examination.

109
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:27,040
Now the tumor actually forms a round mass

110
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:28,140
that's attached to the dura,

111
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:29,520
and that's what imaging would reveal,

112
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:32,260
and that round mass will then press on the cortex,

113
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:33,820
and when it presses on the cortex,

114
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,100
it will create seizures.

115
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,680
Now it's important to note that the tumor compresses,

116
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:39,340
but does not invade the cortex,

117
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:41,560
and again, that'll result in seizures.

118
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:45,600
Here's a classic histology showing the world appearance of cells,

119
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:49,220
which is sort of the high yield characteristic of this tumor.

120
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,480
Remember that this tumor can also result

121
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:52,900
in abundant somoma bodies,

122
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:55,620
and that's because these world cells can calcify,

123
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:56,500
and when they calcify,

124
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:59,380
you would get somoma bodies within the tumor.

125
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:01,580
Now this picture does not show somoma bodies,

126
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:02,920
but it is important to know

127
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:05,800
that you get a world pattern of cells with somoma bodies,

128
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,040
and again, that's high yield.

129
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:10,000
Schwannoma is a tumor that we haven't talked about yet.

130
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:11,960
It's a benign tumor of Schwann cells,

131
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:12,980
and Schwann cells, of course,

132
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,640
are the cells that myelinate the peripheral nervous system.

133
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:19,020
Now this can involve the cranial nerves or the spinal nerves,

134
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:22,060
and it's especially common involving the eighth cranial nerve

135
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:24,080
at the cerebellopontine angle,

136
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:26,360
and so that's why we're including it in this discussion,

137
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:28,300
because when it arises at the CPA,

138
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:29,740
that's going to be within the cranium,

139
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:31,640
and so we're talking about it here

140
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:33,180
as a central nervous system tumor.

141
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:35,720
Patients who have eighth cranial nerve schwannomas,

142
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:37,760
they classically present with a loss of hearing

143
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:39,480
and tinnitus or ringing in their ears.

144
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:42,700
It's important to note that the schwannoma is S100 positive.

145
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:43,520
That's high yield,

146
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:45,060
and it's also high yield to know

147
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:48,100
that bilateral schwannomas of the eighth cranial nerve

148
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:50,560
are seen in neurofibromatosis type 2.

149
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:53,540
Oligodendrogliomas are malignant tumors

150
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:54,920
of oligodendrocytes.

151
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:57,100
The most important association here

152
00:06:57,100 --> 00:07:00,260
is that you get a calcified tumor in the white matter.

153
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:01,420
Of course, it's the white matter

154
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:03,260
because that's where the oligodendrocytes are.

155
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:04,840
They myelinate the axons,

156
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:06,740
producing the color of the white matter,

157
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,780
and it usually involves the frontal lobe

158
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:09,800
presenting with seizures,

159
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:11,440
and so all of this is high yield.

160
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:13,760
Calcified tumor in the frontal lobe,

161
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:15,540
arising in the white matter,

162
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:17,060
and presenting with seizures.

163
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:19,240
Here's an image of the classic histology.

164
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:20,680
It's known as the fried egg appearance.

165
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:22,740
Imagine that this is an egg here.

166
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:24,420
This is the egg white,

167
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:27,440
and then the nucleus represents the yolk of the egg.

168
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:30,260
You can see that there's a bunch of fried eggs

169
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:31,460
throughout this biopsy.

170
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,780
Again, classic for oligodendroglioma.

171
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:37,760
Moving on to tumors that most often arise in children,

172
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:39,320
a pilocytic astrocytoma

173
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:41,240
is a benign tumor of astrocytes.

174
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:43,860
It's the most common CNS tumor in children,

175
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:46,020
and it usually arises in the cerebellum,

176
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:47,060
but of course we know that

177
00:07:47,060 --> 00:07:48,380
because most tumors in children

178
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:50,060
are going to be beneath the tentorium.

179
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:52,680
The classic imaging for a pilocytic astrocytoma

180
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:56,120
is a cystic lesion with a mural nodule,

181
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:58,120
or a nodule growing on its wall,

182
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:01,040
and so this here is the classic imaging.

183
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:02,900
Now, if you have trouble remembering that,

184
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:05,280
you can sort of think as pilocytic astrocytoma

185
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,380
as a pilocytic astrocytoma,

186
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:08,720
if you want to just play with the words,

187
00:08:09,060 --> 00:08:10,180
and then that'll help you to remember

188
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:12,700
that it's a cyst with a mural nodule,

189
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:14,600
and again, this is particularly high yield.

190
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:16,140
When biopsy is performed,

191
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:18,380
the key finding is that you have astrocytes

192
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:21,140
with these thick eosinophilic processes,

193
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:23,360
and these are called Rosenthal fibers,

194
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:25,360
and Rosenthal fibers are an important

195
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:28,900
high yield association with a pilocytic astrocytoma.

196
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:30,360
Now, it's also important to note

197
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,240
that these are astrocytes, which are glial cells,

198
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:35,300
and so this tumor would also be GFAP positive.

199
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:37,660
Medulloblastoma is a malignant tumor

200
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:40,220
derived from the granular cells of the cerebellum.

201
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:42,020
It's particularly high yield to note

202
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:43,180
that this tumor is derived

203
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:45,080
from neural ectodermal tissue.

204
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:46,780
Board examiners like to ask you that.

205
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:48,440
It usually arises in children.

206
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:51,720
The histology reveals small, round blue cells

207
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:53,320
with homo-right rosettes.

208
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:54,940
Now, homo-right rosettes are

209
00:08:54,940 --> 00:08:56,460
when the small, round blue cells,

210
00:08:56,640 --> 00:09:00,120
they wrap around pink areas of neuritic processes,

211
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:02,220
and so we call those homo-right rosettes

212
00:09:02,220 --> 00:09:04,260
because they are forming a rosette

213
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:06,300
around those neuritic processes.

214
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:08,360
This tumor has a poor prognosis.

215
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:09,300
It grows rapidly,

216
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:12,600
and it can then enter into the fourth ventricular space,

217
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:13,980
and using the CSF,

218
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:15,840
it can then drop down to the spinal cord

219
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:17,100
and involve the cord,

220
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:19,240
and those are called drop metastases,

221
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:21,420
and that's also particularly high yield.

222
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,360
Ependymoma is a malignant tumor of the ependymal cells.

223
00:09:24,460 --> 00:09:25,700
Remember that the ependymal cells,

224
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:27,200
they line the ventricular space.

225
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:28,780
It's usually seen in children,

226
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:30,540
and because it's usually seen in children,

227
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:32,040
it'll be beneath the tentorium,

228
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:33,360
and beneath the tentorium means

229
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:34,720
it'll be in the fourth ventricle.

230
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:36,840
Because this tumor arises from the cells

231
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:38,160
lining the ventricular space,

232
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:41,020
it'll eventually grow out into the ventricular space,

233
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:43,180
and it can block the flow of CSF,

234
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:44,880
producing hydrocephalus,

235
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:46,200
and that's very high yield as well.

236
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:48,780
Here's a picture of the histology of an ependymoma.

237
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:50,080
The key finding is that

238
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:52,340
you have perivascular pseudorosettes.

239
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:54,020
Now here's a blood vessel here,

240
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:55,800
and you can see that the tumor cells,

241
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:56,660
these blue cells,

242
00:09:56,720 --> 00:09:59,000
are lining up against this blood vessel

243
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,120
with some pink material in between,

244
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:04,520
and so we call that a perivascular pseudorosette.

245
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:07,080
Cranial pharyngioma is a tumor that arises

246
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,560
from the epithelial remnants of Rathke's pouch.

247
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:11,180
It's particularly high yield

248
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:12,880
because of its tie-in with embryology.

249
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:14,260
So let's go back and review.

250
00:10:14,700 --> 00:10:16,720
Recall that if this is the base of the brain,

251
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:18,280
and this is the floor of the mouth,

252
00:10:18,680 --> 00:10:21,000
then an upward protrusion of the floor of the mouth

253
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,640
results in formation of the anterior pituitary,

254
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:26,400
and a downward protrusion of the base of the brain

255
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:29,480
results in formation of the posterior pituitary.

256
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:32,160
And of course, these will eventually come together

257
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:35,280
to form the anterior and the posterior pituitary.

258
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:38,620
And of course, that sits in a space

259
00:10:38,620 --> 00:10:40,420
called the cella tersica.

260
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:42,200
This is important to note

261
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:44,720
because this upward protrusion of the floor of the mouth

262
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:46,300
is called Rathke's pouch,

263
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:48,080
and eventually it should separate

264
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:49,140
from the floor of the mouth

265
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:52,640
and then produce this distinct anterior pituitary.

266
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:54,700
However, sometimes you can have remnants

267
00:10:54,700 --> 00:10:57,020
of Rathke's pouch that remain along this pathway,

268
00:10:57,260 --> 00:10:58,640
and it's from these remnants

269
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:01,120
that you can develop something called a cranial pharyngioma.

270
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:03,720
Now, think about the word cranial means in the cranium,

271
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:06,700
pharyngi means that you're arising from the pharynx,

272
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:08,580
and oma refers that you have a mass.

273
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:10,800
Now, of course, this tissue here,

274
00:11:11,060 --> 00:11:12,240
the remnants of Rathke's pouch,

275
00:11:12,540 --> 00:11:16,040
arose from the upward protrusion of the pharynx.

276
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:18,160
Now, again, notice that it's sitting here

277
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:19,120
in the cella tersica,

278
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,740
and it can then produce classic symptoms,

279
00:11:21,860 --> 00:11:23,080
which we'll talk about in a moment.

280
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:26,300
Okay, so cranial pharyngioma is a tumor

281
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:29,100
that arises from the epithelial remnants of Rathke's pouch.

282
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:31,780
It presents as a supratentorial mass,

283
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,380
and again, that's because the cella tersica is supratentorial

284
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:36,880
in a child or a young adult.

285
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:39,620
It can compress the optic chiasm,

286
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:41,820
given its location near the optic chiasm,

287
00:11:42,140 --> 00:11:44,920
which will then result in bitemporal hemianopsia.

288
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:45,980
Now, very high yield,

289
00:11:46,040 --> 00:11:47,740
because bitemporal hemianopsia,

290
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:51,340
we sort of always associate with pituitary adenomas,

291
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:53,000
or tumors of the pituitary gland.

292
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:54,460
However, it's important to note

293
00:11:54,460 --> 00:11:56,580
that if you have bitemporal hemianopsia,

294
00:11:56,940 --> 00:11:58,920
in a child, you really want to think

295
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,320
about a cranial pharyngioma,

296
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:02,120
and that's particularly high yield.

297
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:05,200
Now, this tumor on imaging is often calcified,

298
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:06,520
and the way I remember this

299
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:08,200
is that it's derived from the floor of the mouth,

300
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:10,400
and the floor of the mouth has tooth-like tissue,

301
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:12,460
and so you can get calcifications on imaging.

302
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:14,120
The tumor is benign,

303
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:16,100
but it tends to recur after resection,

304
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:18,100
and so these are some of the high yield points

305
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:20,080
concerning cranial pharyngioma,

306
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:21,760
and that concludes our discussion

307
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:23,460
of both the section and the chapter.

